<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- W3C SCXML 6.4 autoforward is owed to the external event, not to the queue
     it arrived on.

     The sibling fixtures pin what is forwarded (`autoforward_done_invoke`,
     name-blind), what is not (`autoforward_internal_queue`, nothing off the
     internal queue), which fields survive (`autoforward_event_fields`) and
     *when* the forward happens (`autoforward_dequeue_point`, one statement
     after the dequeue). All four drive the machine the same way: they let it
     run and the events it forwards are ones it queued for itself. None of
     them can see the question this one asks, because none of them ever hands
     the machine an event from outside.

     A host has two ways to give a running machine an external event: put it
     on the external queue and let the machine drain it, or call the engine's
     own "here is an event" entry point. Appendix D's `mainEventLoop` binds
     the preliminary step to the event, not to the door:

       externalEvent = externalQueue.dequeue()
       datamodel["_event"] = externalEvent
       for state in configuration:
           for inv in state.invoke:
               if inv.invokeid == externalEvent.invokeid:
                   applyFinalize(inv, externalEvent)
               if inv.autoforward:
                   send(inv.id, externalEvent)
       enabledTransitions = selectTransitions(externalEvent)

     An engine that runs that step at one door and not the other satisfies
     every forwarding rule the four sibling fixtures state and still leaves an
     `autoforward` child blind to everything its host delivers — the two
     sessions stop agreeing about what has happened, which is the whole point
     of `autoforward`. Measured 2026-08-21: the C++ AOT engine forwarded a
     dequeued event and dropped an identical one handed to `processEvent()`,
     because the step was written inline in the drain.

     The verdict is decided by a race the child cannot lose either way, so
     neither outcome depends on a timeout and no wall-clock delay is involved:

       inv_probe's onentry sends `ready` to the parent
         -> the parent moves to `armed`, which is where it can be handed an
            event from outside
         -> the host hands the parent `hostPing` through the engine's own
            entry point, NOT through the external queue
         -> the parent's `hostPing` transition sends `marker` straight to the
            child with `<send target="#_inv_probe">`

       forwarded      the child sees hostPing first (the preliminary step runs
                      before transition selection) -> sawHostPing -> pass
       not forwarded  the child only ever sees marker              -> sawMarkerOnly -> fail

     The child opens the exchange, so it is provably alive for everything that
     follows and the fixture does not assume when a backend starts its pending
     invokes. Both child verdicts are top-level `<final>` states, and no
     `<final>` is nested inside a compound state — whether a nested final ends
     a session is a separate question, and mixing the two would let one
     regression masquerade as the other.

     The `<invoke>` sits on `phase` while the arming happens in its children,
     because exiting the state that holds an `<invoke>` cancels it (§6.4) —
     the child has to outlive the handshake that proves it is there.

     Every event is named in full because the AOT backends resolve a forwarded
     event by exact name against the child's own event enum and silently drop
     names it does not declare.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       version="1.0" datamodel="ecmascript" initial="phase"
       name="host_event_reaches_the_child">
    <state id="phase" initial="waiting">
        <invoke type="scxml" id="inv_probe" autoforward="true">
            <content>
                <scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
                       version="1.0" datamodel="ecmascript" initial="watch">
                    <state id="watch">
                        <onentry>
                            <send target="#_parent" event="ready"/>
                        </onentry>
                        <transition event="hostPing" target="forwarded">
                            <send target="#_parent" event="sawHostPing"/>
                        </transition>
                        <transition event="marker" target="unforwarded">
                            <send target="#_parent" event="sawMarkerOnly"/>
                        </transition>
                    </state>
                    <final id="forwarded"/>
                    <final id="unforwarded"/>
                </scxml>
            </content>
        </invoke>
        <state id="waiting">
            <transition event="ready" target="armed"/>
        </state>
        <state id="armed">
            <transition event="hostPing">
                <send target="#_inv_probe" event="marker"/>
            </transition>
        </state>
        <transition event="sawHostPing" target="pass"/>
        <transition event="sawMarkerOnly" target="fail"/>
    </state>
    <final id="pass"/>
    <final id="fail"/>
</scxml>
